WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.199
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:15.286
Unit six: Social Justice. Today you will describe systemic issues with the passive voice.

4
00:00:15.586 --> 00:00:18.180
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.447
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:24.238
Equity.

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.527
Equity means giving people what they need to be equal.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:31.720
Equity means extra help for those who need it.

9
00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:35.317
Your turn. Say: Equity.

10
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.168
Equity.

11
00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:41.556
Systemic inequality.

12
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.303
Systemic inequality means unfairness built into a system.

13
00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:49.648
Systemic inequality affects poor families.

14
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.111
Your turn. Say: Systemic inequality.

15
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:57.711
Systemic inequality.

16
00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.799
Marginalized group.

17
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:04.501
Marginalized group means people who are pushed to the edge of society.

18
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:07.151
Marginalized groups need a voice.

19
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:10.449
Your turn. Say: Marginalized group.

20
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.963
Marginalized group.

21
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:16.915
Human rights.

22
00:01:17.917 --> 00:01:21.019
Human rights means basic rights every person has.

23
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:23.535
Education is a human right.

24
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.698
Your turn. Say: Human rights.

25
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:30.662
Human rights.

26
00:01:31.417 --> 00:01:33.021
Advocacy campaign.

27
00:01:34.017 --> 00:01:37.653
Advocacy campaign means an organised effort to support a cause.

28
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.039
The advocacy campaign reached thousands.

29
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.709
Your turn. Say: Advocacy campaign.

30
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:49.578
Advocacy campaign.

31
00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:51.527
Discrimination.

32
00:01:52.517 --> 00:01:56.467
Discrimination means treating people unfairly because of who they are.

33
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.280
Discrimination is against school rules.

34
00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:02.652
Your turn. Say: Discrimination.

35
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.745
Discrimination.

36
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:08.798
Social movement.

37
00:02:09.783 --> 00:02:13.350
Social movement means many people working for social change.

38
00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.135
The social movement changed the law.

39
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.553
Your turn. Say: Social movement.

40
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.598
Social movement.

41
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:25.908
Inclusive policy.

42
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:30.309
Inclusive policy means a rule that welcomes everyone.

43
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.148
Ramps are part of an inclusive policy.

44
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.449
Your turn. Say: Inclusive policy.

45
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.800
Inclusive policy.

46
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.539
Empowerment.

47
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.780
Empowerment means giving people power over their lives.

48
00:02:47.383 --> 00:02:49.499
Education leads to empowerment.

49
00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:52.736
Your turn. Say: Empowerment.

50
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.601
Empowerment.

51
00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:58.746
Representation.

52
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.864
Representation means having a voice in decisions.

53
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.274
Women need equal representation.

54
00:03:06.883 --> 00:03:09.710
Your turn. Say: Representation.

55
00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:13.986
Representation.

56
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:15.751
Allyship.

57
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.771
Allyship means supporting a group you don't belong to.

58
00:03:20.383 --> 00:03:22.894
Allyship means listening and speaking up.

59
00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:26.089
Your turn. Say: Allyship.

60
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:30.051
Allyship.

61
00:03:30.817 --> 00:03:32.201
Structural change.

62
00:03:33.217 --> 00:03:36.517
Structural change means changing how a system works.

63
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:39.778
Real equality needs structural change.

64
00:03:40.383 --> 00:03:43.419
Your turn. Say: Structural change.

65
00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:47.721
Structural change.

66
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:52.103
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.170
Visal, what causes inequality?

68
00:03:56.283 --> 00:04:00.547
Inequality is caused by structural barriers that must be addressed.

69
00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:02.988
Can you give an example?

70
00:04:04.083 --> 00:04:08.547
In some areas, girls are kept out of school. They are denied their rights.

71
00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:10.918
What can be done?

72
00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:17.592
Inclusive policies should be introduced, and marginalized groups must be heard.

73
00:04:18.733 --> 00:04:20.141
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:20.983 --> 00:04:25.038
Grammar focus: Passive voice for systemic issues.

75
00:04:25.338 --> 00:04:26.443
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.032
Inequality is caused by structural barriers.

77
00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:33.056
Marginalized groups are often ignored.

78
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.973
These barriers must be addressed.

79
00:04:36.933 --> 00:04:42.079
Use the passive to focus on the problem or the people affected, not on who causes it.

80
00:04:42.817 --> 00:04:46.839
Be careful! Don't say: Inequality is cause by barriers.

81
00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.798
Say: Inequality is caused by barriers.

82
00:04:52.098 --> 00:04:54.109
The passive needs the past participle.

83
00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.367
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:04:58.167 --> 00:04:59.156
Fair for All.

85
00:04:59.456 --> 00:05:02.129
Social justice means fairness for everyone.

86
00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:06.840
In many places, inequality is caused by structural barriers.

87
00:05:07.833 --> 00:05:11.017
Marginalized groups are often denied their human rights.

88
00:05:12.033 --> 00:05:15.473
Advocacy campaigns and social movements fight discrimination.

89
00:05:16.467 --> 00:05:20.672
Through allyship and inclusive policy, structural change is possible.

90
00:05:21.767 --> 00:05:26.256
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:27.117 --> 00:05:29.430
Inequality is blank by barriers.

92
00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.244
A, caused. B, cause. C, causing.

93
00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:38.916
The answer is A: caused.

94
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.443
Treating people unfairly because of who they are is blank.

95
00:05:43.743 --> 00:05:48.267
A, discrimination. B, equity. C, allyship.

96
00:05:51.833 --> 00:05:54.413
The answer is A: discrimination.

97
00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:57.944
Supporting a group you don't belong to is blank.

98
00:05:58.244 --> 00:06:03.175
A, allyship. B, empowerment. C, representation.

99
00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.570
The answer is A: allyship.

100
00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:12.941
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:13.783 --> 00:06:16.909
What social justice issue matters most to you and why?

102
00:06:25.717 --> 00:06:27.205
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:27.505 --> 00:06:37.240
Equal education matters most to me. In poor areas, many children are kept out of school, so free schools should be built for them.

104
00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.214
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:40.117 --> 00:06:45.277
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and passive voice for systemic issues.

106
00:06:45.577 --> 00:06:51.146
Today's value is equality. Treat everyone fairly. We all deserve the same chances.

107
00:06:51.446 --> 00:06:53.970
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

108
00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:57.352
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

